  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions continue through \nTuesday. Precipitation chances return late next week. \n\n1033-1035 mb surface high pressure will build in from the north \ntoday before meandering over the Chesapeake Bay/Delmarva Sunday into \nMonday. As a result, expect light surface winds out of the north and \nnorthwest today switching to the south Sunday. Temperatures will \nremain seasonable throughout the weekend with highs in the 60s (50s \nmountains) and lows in the 40s (30s valleys).\n\nHigh pressure will gradually move offshore Tuesday into Wednesday as \na deep upper level trough approaches from the Tennessee/lower Ohio \nRiver Valleys. Guidance continues to show numerous pieces of \nshortwave energy expected to traverse the base of this trough during \nthis period. Additionally, there is indication that a cutoff low \nwill develop over the southeastern CONUS ahead of this trough \npattern. The interaction of these systems is likely to be complex, \nwith it being hard to tell exactly where all of the pieces line up \nat this stage.The GFS has at least two cutoff lows along the trough \nitself while the Canadian and Euro have a low along the trough and \noff the coastal Carolinas. As for timing, the GFS and Canadian are \nslower compared to the Euro counterpart. With that said, expect dry \nconditions to continue through Wednesday as high pressure retreats \nfurther offshore allowing the ridge to flatten/mid-level dry air to \nerode. This will allow precipitation chances to increase from the \nsouthwest late Wednesday with widespread rainfall Thursday into \nFriday. High pressure will quickly return behind the system Friday \ninto the upcoming weekend before a more potent trough/push of cold \nair follows beyond the period. \n\nMonday through Thursday: High temperatures will be in the upper 60s \nto low 70s east of the Blue Ridge with those along and west in the \nupper 50s to 60s. Overnight low temperatures will be in the 50s each \nnight.",
